  "comment": "Shared lexical denylist vectors. Run by crates/sj-core/tests/core_tests.rs (is_protected_path) and packages/core/test/safety.test.mjs (isProtectedPath). Every path is lexical and nonexistent; home is a synthetic placeholder. Add a vector here for every denylist bug so both implementations stay in lockstep. Every rule of the former JS denylist (main a66538f packages/core/src/protect.mjs) has a vector here; the Rust gate is the only implementation. Sections: protected/unprotected run as pure string tests on every platform; posix_host_protected needs POSIX path semantics (drive letters are meaningless there). Note: there was no config/protected.json on main. fs_cases (Unix only) are built under a scratch root: paths are relative to it, 'home' is the synthetic home, link entries are symlinks (target relative to the root unless absolute), options mirror --exact/--dynamic/--glob; a candidate listed under protected must be judged protected, under unprotected must not. A fs_case with 'cwd' (relative to the scratch root) runs with that working directory: relative custom rules are anchored to the cwd exactly like main's path.resolve did, then resolved in OS order (symlink target first, then ..); the Rust core harness skips cwd cases (a process cwd is not thread safe) and crates/sj-cli/tests/cli_tests.rs runs them through the binary.",
